<binaryimage arch="sparc:BE:64:default:default">
<!--
    GCC -O0 on SPARC V9 64-bit spills an int argument and reads its low byte
    back through an OR-masked frame address (sample_branch's `n` spill):

        int flag(int n) { return n & 1; }

        save    %sp, -0xb0, %sp
        stw     %i0, [%fp+0x7fb]
        add     %fp, 0x7fb, %i0
        or      %i0, 3, %i0          ! low byte of the big-endian int
        ldub    [%i0], %i0
        and     %i0, 1, %i0
        ret
        restore

    Two defects removed the spill, so the result read a slot nothing wrote:
    the indexed-stack-pointer walk did not follow INT_OR, so the LOAD got no
    guard; and SparcV9_64.cspec spelled its negative local range in 32 bits,
    so the open array at 0x7fb ran past the frame into the parameter range,
    markUnaliased called it unaliased, and the guard's address force on the
    spill was cleared. Hand-assembled.
-->
<bytechunk space="ram" offset="0x100000" readonly="true">
9de3bf50f027a7fbb007a7fbb0162003f00e0000b00e200181c7e00881e80000
</bytechunk>
<symbol space="ram" offset="0x100000" name="flag"/>
</binaryimage>
<script>
  <com>map prototype flag int flag(int n);</com>
  <com>lo fu flag</com>
  <com>decompile</com>
  <com>print C</com>
  <com>quit</com>
</script>
<stringmatch name="sparc64-or-masked-spill #1: the argument spill is kept" min="1" max="1">v1\[0\] = n;</stringmatch>
<stringmatch name="sparc64-or-masked-spill #2: the byte is read back through the spilled local" min="1" max="1">return \*\(uint1 \*\)\(\(uint8\)v1 \| 3\) &amp; 1;</stringmatch>
</decompilertest>
